 "claim": "The splitting lemma: in a space made of a base and atoms (the Fano plane or census 58 minus one element) at 3-edge cuts, whether removing a line M creates at a gluing line L depends only on the atoms holding M and L, because every other atom acts as the vertex it replaced. Proved for untouched atoms attached at a point (interface lemma: they leave their ports exactly the two constant patterns, checked for every element; screening lemma); for untouched atoms attached at a line the solutions are unchanged (their splits allow exactly the six other patterns) and creation is checked. Exact on thirteen designs with two or three atoms, including adjacent and touching placements and atoms attached to the Fano plane and census 58: every entry agrees with the space keeping only the touched atoms, and plane glue alone never creates. So each removal's creation in the glue is its atom's halo with that atom alone, atoms attached at lines have no halo, and glue removals see each atom as if it were alone. The creation map, taken over the four far sides, does not depend on the order of the gluing line's points (786 of 786 cases), though the census-6 far side alone does.",
